freedreno/turnip: round-robin wave dispatch wherever the hardware does not charge for it.

Round-robin exists for persistent-thread compute. Such a shader spins pulling
work from a queue, and every wave has to keep advancing -- without fairness one
that will not yield can starve the rest, and the loop stops making progress.

Today it arrives one shader at a time. dxil-spirv signals the need per shader
(`NonSemantic.dxil-spirv.quirks` -> `spirv_to_nir.c` ->
`occupancy_bounded_workgroup_fairness` -> `ir3_shader.c` ->
`cs.round_robin_mode`), which only helps a title somebody has already diagnosed
and written a quirk for. Globally it is reachable only through
`TU_DEBUG=COMPUTE_ROUND_ROBIN`, which is to say not at all in a shipped build.

**Everything expensive about the mode is gated on `round_robin_errata`, not on
the mode itself.** `ir3.c:305` caps occupancy at 8 waves and calls `exit(1)` for
a barrier shader that needs more than that; `ir3_get_min_reg_count` inflates the
minimum register count to enforce the cap. Both check the errata bit first and
return immediately when it is clear:

    if (v->cs.round_robin_mode && compiler->info->props.round_robin_errata) {
       if (waves_per_wg > 8 && v->has_barrier) { ...; exit(1); }
       max_waves = MIN2(max_waves, 8);
    }

a8xx sets `round_robin_errata = False` (`freedreno_devices.py:1377`). None of
those paths is taken there and the mode reduces to a single register write --
`tu_shader.cc`, `computerrmodeen`. The driver charges nothing.

That gating is also the explanation for the current default. On a6xx and a7xx,
where the errata is set, turning this on globally really would be costly, so
`TU_DEBUG` is the right home for it *there*. Keyed on the errata rather than on
the debug flag, it turns on exactly where it is free and stays off exactly where
it is not. Nothing changes for any generation that has the errata.

**What is not established is the hardware-side cost**, and this patch does not
claim otherwise. Interleaving waves instead of running them to completion can
cost cache locality, which is invisible from the source and would show up as
frame rate. If a title regresses, this is the first line to put back.

The immediate motivation is `docs/DEBUGGING.md` #56: RE Engine's culling shaders
are persistent-thread loops, and upstream pairs their wave-size quirk with
`ENABLE_FAIR_SCHEDULING` for exactly this reason. Every other engine doing the
same thing on this device currently gets nothing, because nobody has written it
a quirk.

diff --git a/src/freedreno/vulkan/tu_device.cc b/src/freedreno/vulkan/tu_device.cc
